  "situation": "In early 1963, the Secretary of Defense challenged the plausibility of estimates of Soviet ground-force strength and asked intelligence leadership to reexamine them. The 1962 estimate described about 80 combat-ready divisions and another 65 requiring substantial augmentation, and projected about 100 combat-ready divisions and 125 less-prepared divisions after 30 days of mobilization. Earlier estimates had put the Soviet Army at 175 active divisions, with another 125 available within 30 days. Analysts had limited ability to confirm the continued existence of many units and had uncertainty about organizational changes and current standards. Satellite coverage could help establish the existence of most division-sized units, but its early quality was rudimentary, and personnel-strength assessments relied on fragmented information. New photographic, documentary, and human-source information was available for assessing the estimate.",
